WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.858 --> 00:00:03.761
Line 3 in hall B needs a heavier connection.

2
00:00:04.479 --> 00:00:05.594
It is not there yet.

3
00:00:06.677 --> 00:00:09.521
A grid planner finds out why it has to wait.

4
00:00:10.553 --> 00:00:14.663
All grid planning starts with how much power will flow at the same moment.

5
00:00:15.713 --> 00:00:17.385
Four topics follow his work.

6
00:00:18.107 --> 00:00:21.265
First what is coming and whether the grid survives a failure.

7
00:00:22.353 --> 00:00:24.814
Then what gets built and how you buy time.

8
00:00:26.114 --> 00:00:28.517
The first topic is Load and forecasting.

9
00:00:29.266 --> 00:00:33.538
Simultaneity is the share of everyone’s maximum that occurs at once.

10
00:00:34.563 --> 00:00:36.014
Then the planner looks ahead.

11
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:39.646
Demand grows and line 3 comes on top.

12
00:00:40.371 --> 00:00:42.147
So the limit comes into view.

13
00:00:43.134 --> 00:00:47.906
He runs a load flow and sees what current runs through the cable to hall B.

14
00:00:49.192 --> 00:00:52.408
The second topic is Network design and redundancy.

15
00:00:53.071 --> 00:00:55.242
Every design passes four checks.

16
00:00:55.956 --> 00:00:58.650
One asks what happens when something fails.

17
00:00:59.651 --> 00:01:00.963
A ring gives reserve.

18
00:01:01.709 --> 00:01:05.285
If a cable fails, the other side feeds hall B.

19
00:01:06.249 --> 00:01:10.045
For the voltage check he shares out the voltage budget across the grid.

20
00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.609
The third topic is Investment planning.

21
00:01:14.346 --> 00:01:16.749
There are more bottlenecks than people and money.

22
00:01:17.728 --> 00:01:22.082
So he ranks them by risk, waiting customers and how ready the solution is.

23
00:01:23.066 --> 00:01:26.328
So the connection of line 3 may have to wait its turn.

24
00:01:27.344 --> 00:01:30.351
He records the scenario in the investment plan.

25
00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:35.267
The fourth topic is Flexibility and alternatives to building.

26
00:01:35.988 --> 00:01:38.172
Can it work without building, or later?

27
00:01:39.183 --> 00:01:42.480
A limit may be hit only a few hundred hours a year.

28
00:01:43.173 --> 00:01:45.181
Taking a little less then makes room.

29
00:01:46.171 --> 00:01:49.073
For that he offers a capacity steering contract.

30
00:01:50.102 --> 00:01:51.205
That buys time.

31
00:01:51.888 --> 00:01:55.650
So line 3 can already run while the heavier connection waits.

32
00:01:56.947 --> 00:01:57.702
The whole map.

33
00:01:58.444 --> 00:02:02.949
Grid planning and the transition is the seventh station of Energy and the grid.

34
00:02:03.992 --> 00:02:05.722
Every topic has four levels.

35
00:02:06.741 --> 00:02:09.551
Start with From home to station at Basics level.

36
00:02:10.343 --> 00:02:12.735
That is where all grid planning begins.

37
00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:16.843
At Working knowledge level come current and voltage,

38
00:02:17.005 --> 00:02:19.826
the ring, bottlenecks and congestion management.

39
00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:26.376
At Advanced level come the forecast per station, N-1, prioritising and batteries.

40
00:02:27.411 --> 00:02:29.698
At Expert level come time series,

41
00:02:29.884 --> 00:02:34.168
risk-based design, from plan to built grid and the value of deferral.

42
00:02:35.243 --> 00:02:39.423
Every lesson and every film in this module has a place on this map.
